","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Xi_Jinping_on_August_18%2C_2026_(cropped).jpg/330px-Xi_Jinping_on_August_18%2C_2026_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1953-06-15","died":null,"birthplace":"Beijing","parties":["Chinese Communist Party"],"education":["Tsinghua University","Beijing Bayi School","Beijing 101 Middle School"],"occupations":["statesperson","politician"],"wiki":"Xi Jinping"},"Q18111":{"id":"Q18111","name":"Li Keqiang","desc":"Premier of the People's Republic of China from 2013 to 2023","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Li_Keqiang_(20230104).jpg/330px-Li_Keqiang_(20230104).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1955-07-03","died":"2023-10-27","birthplace":"Hefei","parties":["Chinese Communist Party"],"education":["Peking University","Hefei No. 8 High School","Peking University","Hefei Nanmen Primary School"],"occupations":["politician","economist","jurist"],"wiki":"Li Keqiang"},"Q4414390":{"id":"Q4414390","name":"Li Qiang","desc":"Premier of the People's Republic of China since 2023","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Li_Qiang%2C_Premier_of_the_People's_Republic_of_China_on_October_27%2C_2025_(cropped).jpg/330px-Li_Qiang%2C_Premier_of_the_People's_Republic_of_China_on_October_27%2C_2025_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1959-07-23","died":null,"birthplace":"Rui'an","parties":["Chinese Communist Party"],"education":["Central Party School","Zhejiang Agricultural University","Hong Kong Polytechnic University"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Li Qiang"}},"summary":"China, officially the People's Republic of China (PRC), is a country in East Asia. It is divided into 33 province-level divisions, including two special administrative regions. Beijing is the capital, while Shanghai is the most populous city by urban area. Its geography features the Central Plain, major rivers such as the Yangtze and Yellow River, deserts, subtropical and temperate forests, plateaus, and mountain ranges such as the Himalayas. It is the world's second-most populous country after India, with a population exceeding 1.4 billion (17% of the world's population). Covering 9.6 million square kilometers (3,700,000 sq mi), it is the third largest country by area.\nHumans first arrived in China during the Paleolithic Age. During the 2nd millennium BCE, dynastic states emerged. In 221 BCE, China was unified under the Qin, followed by the Han dynasty, ushering in two millennia of imperial rule across periods of unity and division. The imperial era saw the expansion of the Silk Road and the invention of gunpowder, paper, printing, and the compass. After increased Western political, economic, and philosophical influence, the empire was succeeded by the Republic of China (ROC) after the 1911 Revolution. The Warlord Era and Chinese Civil War followed, interrupted by the Second Sino-Japanese War, which ended in a Chinese victory. In 1949, the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) proclaimed the PRC and the ROC retreated to Taiwan. Both sides still claim political legitimacy. Attempts by the CCP to advance its political program faltered through famine and political turmoil. The reform and opening-up that began in 1978 moved China towards a socialist market economy, spurring economic growth.\nChina is a unitary state with the CCP as its sole ruling party. It is one of the five permanent members of the UN Security Council and a member of many multilateral organizations. China is the world's largest manufacturer and has the second-largest economy by nominal GDP. International organizations have criticized China, alleging authoritarianism and human rights abuses. Possessing a large military, space program, and nuclear stockpile, China has been described as a superpower due to its influence in geopolitics, science and technology, manufacturing, economics, and culture.\nChinese art and culture have influenced much of Asia, with a literary tradition dating back millennia to the Chinese classics. China is the birthplace of Confucianism and Daoism, which form the traditional three teachings of Chinese philosophy and folk religion alongside Buddhism. Chinese cuisine is diverse and highly regional, with rice as a staple in the south and wheat in the north. It has 60 World Heritage Sites, including the Great Wall and Grand Canal. The Han, speakers of Sinitic languages, are China's dominant ethnicity, although it is home to 55 recognized minorities, including the Hui, Mongols, Tibetans, Uyghurs, and Zhuang.","history":"The history of China spans several millennia across a wide geographical area. Chinese civilization originated in the Yellow and Yangtze river basins, evolving into one of the world's oldest cradles of civilization. Traditional historiography views this past through the lens of the dynastic cycle. The earliest confirmed historical period begins with the Shang dynasty (c. 1250 BC), which left the oldest surviving oracle bone inscriptions. It was followed by the Zhou dynasty (c. 1046 – 256 BC), an era that introduced the Mandate of Heaven and laid the philosophical foundations for Confucianism, Taoism, and Legalism.\nChina was first unified under a centralized imperial state by Qin Shi Huang in 221 BC. The subsequent Han dynasty (202 BC – 220 AD) established a long-lasting imperial order, institutionalized Confucianism, and fostered major technological advancements such as paper-making. Following centuries of disunity that saw the spread of Buddhism, imperial unity was restored by the Sui dynasty (581–618) and the Tang dynasty (618–907), a golden age of poetry, culture, and cosmopolitan trade. The Song dynasty (960–1279) followed, driving major advancements in woodblock printing, technology, and the imperial examination system.\nIn the 13th century, the Mongol Empire conquered China and established the Yuan dynasty (1271–1368). Han rule returned with the Ming dynasty (1368–1644), noted for maritime exploration, arts, and major restoration of the Great Wall and Grand Canal. The final imperial lineage, the Manchu-led Qing dynasty (1644–1912), expanded China's territory to its imperial peak before suffering severe internal instability and military defeats to foreign powers in the 19th century.\nThe 1911 Revolution ended imperial rule and established the Republic of China (ROC). Decades of conflict followed, including the Second Sino-Japanese War and the Chinese Civil War. In 1949, Mao Zedong, leader of the Chinese Communist Party, proclaimed the People's Republic of China (PRC) on the mainland, while the ROC government retreated to Taiwan. Following the Cultural Revolution, the PRC instituted reform and opening up under Deng Xiaoping starting in 1978, transforming the country into the world's fastest-growing major economy.","historyTitle":"History of China","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,667070000],[1961,660330000],[1962,665770000],[1963,682335000],[1964,698355000],[1965,715185000],[1966,735400000],[1967,754550000],[1968,774510000],[1969,796025000],[1970,818315000],[1971,841105000],[1972,862030000],[1973,881940000],[1974,900350000],[1975,916395000],[1976,930685000],[1977,943455000],[1978,956165000],[1979,969005000],[1980,981235000],[1981,993885000],[1982,1008630000],[1983,1023310000],[1984,1036830000],[1985,1051040000],[1986,1066790000],[1987,1084040000],[1988,1101630000],[1989,1118650000],[1990,1135190000],[1991,1150780000],[1992,1164970000],[1993,1178440000],[1994,1191840000],[1995,1204860000],[1996,1217550000],[1997,1230080000],[1998,1241940000],[1999,1252740000],[2000,1262650000],[2001,1271850000],[2002,1280400000],[2003,1288400000],[2004,1296080000],[2005,1303720000],[2006,1311020000],[2007,1317890000],[2008,1324660000],[2009,1331260000],[2010,1337710000],[2011,1345040000],[2012,1354190000],[2013,1363240000],[2014,1371860000],[2015,1379860000],[2016,1387790000],[2017,1396220000],[2018,1402760000],[2019,1407750000],[2020,1411100000],[2021,1412360000],[2022,1412180000],[2023,1410710000],